# 6. H6 — NTLM Network Authentication Compatible with Pass-the-Hash

## 6.1 What H6 Tries to Prove

**H6** tries to prove that `DC01` received NTLM network authentication from the controlled attacker host and that the authenticated account received special privileges.

In natural language:

> **If `DC01` records NTLM network authentication from `192.168.56.40` and the account receives special privileges, then there is a signal compatible with credential-based lateral movement and possible Pass-the-Hash activity.**

## 6.2 Expected NTLM Indicators

| Field | Expected Value |
|---|---|
| `agent.name` | `DC01` |
| `data.win.system.eventID` | `4624` |
| `data.win.eventdata.logonType` | `3` |
| `data.win.eventdata.authenticationPackageName` | `NTLM` |
| `data.win.eventdata.ipAddress` | `192.168.56.40` |
| `data.win.eventdata.targetUserName` | `<privileged_user>` |
| `rule.id` | `100011` |
| `rule.mitre.id` | `T1021.002`, `T1550.002` |

## 6.3 Expected Privilege Indicators

| Field | Expected Value |
|---|---|
| `agent.name` | `DC01` |
| `data.win.system.eventID` | `4672` |
| `data.win.eventdata.subjectUserName` | `<privileged_user>` |
| `data.win.eventdata.privilegeList` | Administrative privileges |
| `rule.id` | `100021` |
| `rule.mitre.id` | `T1078`, `T1550.002` |

## 6.4 Detection Value

H6 provides an early signal.

It tells the analyst:

```text
A privileged account authenticated to DC01 over the network using NTLM.
```

This is compatible with the effect of Pass-the-Hash because the technique produces NTLM authentication behavior.

## 6.5 Limitation

H6 does **not** prove Pass-the-Hash by itself.

A normal NTLM authentication using a real password may look similar in Windows logs.

Wazuh does not know whether:

```text
the user typed a password
```

or:

```text
an attacker reused an NTLM hash
```

Therefore, H6 should be treated as:

```text
credential-based lateral movement indicator
```

not as:

```text
direct proof of hash reuse
```

