# 7. H7 — Pass-the-Hash / DCSync Correlation

## 7.1 What H7 Tries to Prove

**H7** tries to prove that credential access behavior and privileged NTLM access appeared as part of the same controlled attack chain.

In natural language:

> **If `DC01` records DCSync-like activity by a non-machine account and this is correlated with privileged access inside a defined timeframe, then the chain is compatible with Pass-the-Hash / credential access behavior.**

## 7.2 Expected DCSync Indicators

| Field | Expected Value |
|---|---|
| `agent.name` | `DC01` |
| `data.win.system.eventID` | `4662` |
| `data.win.eventdata.accessMask` | `0x100` |
| `data.win.eventdata.properties` | Replication-related GUIDs or permissions |
| `data.win.eventdata.subjectUserName` | Non-machine account |
| `rule.id` | `100030` |
| `rule.mitre.id` | `T1003.006` |

## 7.3 Expected Correlation Indicators

| Field | Expected Value |
|---|---|
| Previous signal | `100030` |
| Current signal | `100021` |
| Correlation rule | `100033` |
| Time window | `300` seconds |
| Severity | Level `15` |
| MITRE mapping | `T1550.002`, `T1003.006` |

## 7.4 Detection Value

H7 is the strongest hypothesis in the Pass-the-Hash scenario.

It does not rely on a single NTLM logon.

It links:

```text
DCSync-like activity → privileged access
```

That is why it has higher analytical value than `100011` alone.

## 7.5 Limitation

Even H7 must be interpreted carefully.

The SIEM still does not observe the hash directly.

The correct wording is:

```text
behavior compatible with Pass-the-Hash
```

not:

```text
direct proof that the hash was used
```

